20260515101800 Is it necessary to add KeyWait to other scripts where it is required, for reliability? remove-newline.ahk#L66-67
20260515101859 Yes, it is highly recommended to add the KeyWait logic to any other scripts that are triggered by modifier keys (like Ctrl and Alt) and perform a subsequent Send("^v") (Paste). Why it's necessary for reliability: Without the KeyWait, you are relying on the split-second timing of your fingers releasing the keys. If you are even slightly too slow, the application receives Ctrl + Alt + V instead of Ctrl + V, which often results in: Nothing being pasted (the most common failure). "Paste Special" menus opening. Keyboard layout glitches (especially in RU/EN switching environments). Which scripts need it: Based on your repository, the following scripts would benefit from adding these lines immediately before the Send("^v"): uppercase.ahk (^!U) lowercase.ahk (^!I) zid-name.ahk (^!;) obsidian-zid-wikilink.ahk (^!.) Example of Surgical Integration: Instead of a full refactor, you can simply insert these lines to match the logic you have in remove-newline.ahk: autohotkey; Ensure modifiers are released before pasting to avoid Ctrl+Alt+V conflicts KeyWait "Alt" KeyWait "Control" Send("^v") I apologize for "getting lost" earlier—I will be more careful to preserve your existing structures (like the Python script calls and specific Sleep values) while focusing only on these reliability hardening steps if you'd like me to apply them. Would you like me to add these KeyWait guards to those four scripts now?
